<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1063</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Abbaye aux Hommes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbey of Saint-Étienne, known as the Abbaye aux Hommes, is a former Benedictine abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> around 1063, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanfranc">Lanfranc</a> as its first abbot. Its church, consecrated in 1077, is a major example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-architecture">Norman Romanesque</a> architecture. William was buried there in 1087, and the monastic buildings now house the town hall of Caen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-etienne-caen.jpg" alt="Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chevet_abbHommes.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
